Kent Yoder wrote on=A02012-10-10:
> On Tue, Oct 09, 2012 at 05:35:22PM +0800, gang.wei@intel.com wrote:
>> @@ -1476,7 +1477,7 @@ struct tpm_chip *tpm_register_hardware(struct
> device *dev,
>>  		goto put_device;
>>  	}
>> -	if (sys_add_ppi(&dev->kobj)) {
>> +	if (tpm_add_ppi(&dev->kobj)) {
>>  		misc_deregister(&chip->vendor.miscdev);
>>  		goto put_device;
>>  	}
>> =20
>   Hmm, tpm_add_ppi is just sysfs_create_group, which only ever returns
> 0. Looks like we can remove this error path, but PPI is unusable in =
the
> failure case.

sysfs_create_group will return 0 on success or return error code. So I =
don't
think we can remove this error path. The previous call to =
sysfs_create_group
also have similar error path.

>> +EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(tpm_add_ppi);
>> ...
>> +EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(tpm_remove_ppi);
>>=20
>  Do we need to export these symbols?  These might have been left =
around
> from when ppi was a standalone module.

We definitely need to export these symbols, since ppi was in =
tpm_bios.ko,
and these symbols are called from tpm.ko.

Jimmy

